From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 22:45:31 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] x87: FPREM and FPREM1 compute x87's exact remainders

FPREM computed its remainder in one step through a rounded quotient,
which is wrong once the quotient no longer fits the significand, and
set no quotient bits; x86 reduces operands whose exponents differ by 64
or more only partly and reports that in C2, and otherwise sets C0, C3
and C1 to the quotient's low three bits. FPREM1 used SoftFloat's IEEE
remainder, right in value but without the bits.

A new SoftFloat routine, extF80_x87Rem, computes both exactly with
integer division of the significands: a complete remainder for an
exponent difference D below 64, the quotient truncated for FPREM and
rounded to nearest even for FPREM1; for D of 64 or more a reduction by
the divisor scaled to N = 32 + D mod 32 bits below the dividend,
truncated for both, with C2 set. Denormal operands count by their
normalized exponents, a pseudo-denormal dividend comes back
normalized, an exact zero takes the dividend's sign, and a quiet NaN
propagates without raising invalid operation. This matches 6588
results of a Xeon 8581C, denormal operands and partial remainders
included.

The FPREM helpers set C0 to C3 after the exception handling, unless an
unmasked invalid operation or denormal operand leaves them as they
were, and the dispatcher no longer clears C2 after them. The reduced
precision path, which an embedding host turns off, now leaves C2 as
it was.
